tered points. ","_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Scatter points is scattering points on our dummy geometry. \n\nBy default it creates orientations from the mesh you scatter on, either based on UVs or connectivity. We use those orientations in this example. \n\nThe local Y axis of the instance points along the normal.  The local Z axis points along the tangent. \n\nTangents can be based on the mesh's structure (connectivity) or the mesh's UV coordinates. You can also provide your own data for normals or tangents. "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Create instances puts instances on points. An \"instances\" object is a kind of geometry, closely related to \"points\". In fact instances are still a points object, and can be manipulated like points. \n\nWhen no instance geometry is provided to \"create instances\",  the default instanced geometry is a colorful sphere. \n\nIn this case we are instancing axes geometry built from cubes, which  visualizes our orientations and scales. "}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Double-click for more info"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Scatter points"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Instances"}]},"ports":[{"portName":"scatter_examples","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"dummy_geo","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"true"}],"node_count":4,"connection_count":5,"value_count":7},{"path":"root/dummy_geomery","name":"dummy_geomery","metadata":{"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Create a Sphere"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Create a displacement field"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Displace and output"}]},"ports":[{"portName":"out_points","portDirection":"output"},{"portName":"noise_scale","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"}],"terminal_states":[],"node_count":7,"connection_count":9,"value_count":18},{"path":"root/gnomon","name":"gnomon","metadata":{},"ports":[{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"nubbin_size","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"},{"portName":"stem_size","portDirection":"input","portType":"float"}],"terminal_states":[],"node_count":17,"connection_count":36,"value_count":54},{"path":"root/gnomon/add_center_color","name":"add_center_color","metadata":{},"ports":[{"portName":"point_position","portDirection":"input","portType":"array<Math::float3>"},{"portName":"output","portDirection":"output"},{"portName":"center_color","portDirection":"input","portType":"Math::float3"}],"terminal_states":[],"node_count":6,"connection_count":11,"value_count":0}],"teaching_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Scatter points is scattering points on our dummy geometry. \n\nBy default it creates orientations from the mesh you scatter on, either based on UVs or connectivity. We use those orientations in this example. \n\nThe local Y axis of the instance points along the normal.  The local Z axis points along the tangent. \n\nTangents can be based on the mesh's structure (connectivity) or the mesh's UV coordinates. You can also provide your own data for normals or tangents. "},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Create instances puts instances on points. An \"instances\" object is a kind of geometry, closely related to \"points\". In fact instances are still a points object, and can be manipulated like points. \n\nWhen no instance geometry is provided to \"create instances\",  the default instanced geometry is a colorful sphere. \n\nIn this case we are instancing axes geometry built from cubes, which  visualizes our orientations and scales. "}]}